From: Wolf HDR integration <noreply@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] wayland: expose HDR capabilities negotiated with parent

The nested Wayland backend receives an image-description feedback object from
its parent compositor.  In Wolf HDR sessions that feedback advertises both
BT.2020 and ST.2084/PQ, but KWin 6.6 still only reports DPMS/tearing
capabilities to KScreen.  Consequently Plasma hides the HDR and Wide Color
Gamut controls even though KWin can create and attach HDR image descriptions
to the parent surface.

Advertise those capabilities only when the parent color-management protocol
explicitly supports the required BT.2020/PQ primitives.  Also retain the
desktop's requested HDR/WCG state in the Wayland output, matching the DRM
backend's configuration semantics.  This does not invent a display mode: the
parent compositor remains the authority for the negotiated color description.

---
 src/backends/wayland/wayland_output.cpp | 21 +++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/backends/wayland/wayland_output.cpp b/src/backends/wayland/wayland_output.cpp
index 1a5a15ea9..f1c5b1b8e 100644
--- a/src/backends/wayland/wayland_output.cpp
+++ b/src/backends/wayland/wayland_output.cpp
@@ -128,4 +128,11 @@ WaylandOutput::WaylandOutput(const QString &name, WaylandBackend *backend)
     , m_backend(backend)
     , m_cursor(std::make_unique<WaylandCursor>(backend))
 {
+    const int initialRefreshHz = qEnvironmentVariableIntValue("KWIN_WAYLAND_REFRESH");
+    if (initialRefreshHz > 0 && initialRefreshHz <= 1000) {
+        // A nested parent may not implement presentation-time feedback.  In
+        // that case use the stream rate supplied by the embedding session
+        // instead of silently exposing KWin's 60Hz fallback mode.
+        m_refreshRate = initialRefreshHz * 1000;
+    }
     m_renderLoop->setMaxPendingFrameCount(2);
@@ -140,6 +147,14 @@ WaylandOutput::WaylandOutput(const QString &name, WaylandBackend *backend)
             && manager->supportsFeature(WP_COLOR_MANAGER_V1_FEATURE_SET_LUMINANCES)
             && manager->supportsTransferFunction(WP_COLOR_MANAGER_V1_TRANSFER_FUNCTION_GAMMA22);
         if (supportsMinFeatures) {
+            const bool supportsHdrOutput = manager->supportsPrimaries(WP_COLOR_MANAGER_V1_PRIMARIES_BT2020)
+                && manager->supportsTransferFunction(WP_COLOR_MANAGER_V1_TRANSFER_FUNCTION_ST2084_PQ);
+            if (supportsHdrOutput) {
+                // A nested output has no EDID/DRM connector to inspect.  The
+                // parent color-management protocol is its authoritative HDR
+                // capability source instead.
+                caps |= Capability::WideColorGamut | Capability::HighDynamicRange;
+            }
             m_colorSurfaceFeedback = std::make_unique<ColorSurfaceFeedback>(wp_color_manager_v1_get_surface_feedback(manager->object(), *m_surface));
             connect(m_colorSurfaceFeedback.get(), &ColorSurfaceFeedback::preferredColorChanged, this, &WaylandOutput::updateColor);
         }
@@ -381,6 +396,12 @@ void WaylandOutput::applyChanges(const OutputConfiguration &config)
     next.desiredModeSize = props->desiredModeSize.value_or(m_state.desiredModeSize);
     next.desiredModeRefreshRate = props->desiredModeRefreshRate.value_or(m_state.desiredModeRefreshRate);
     next.desiredModeFlags = props->desiredModeFlags.value_or(m_state.desiredModeFlags);
+    if (capabilities() & Capability::HighDynamicRange) {
+        next.highDynamicRange = props->highDynamicRange.value_or(m_state.highDynamicRange);
+    }
+    if (capabilities() & Capability::WideColorGamut) {
+        next.wideColorGamut = props->wideColorGamut.value_or(m_state.wideColorGamut);
+    }
     next.uuid = props->uuid.value_or(m_state.uuid);
     next.replicationSource = props->replicationSource.value_or(m_state.replicationSource);
     next.dpmsMode = props->dpmsMode.value_or(m_state.dpmsMode);
